// RUN: cudaq-opt --canonicalize='region-simplify=aggressive' %s | FileCheck %s

// *** IF THIS TEST FAILS, YOUR MLIR/LLVM BUILD IS MISSING THE CUDA-Q PATCHES. ***
//
// Quake's linear types (!quake.wire, !quake.cable) carry the `SSIType` trait.
// A patched MLIR honors that trait and will not collapse block arguments (or
// region successor inputs) of those types when every predecessor forwards the
// same value. An unpatched MLIR treats wires as plain SSA values, folds the
// block arguments away, and silently turns each wire into a value with
// multiple uses, which is invalid linear IR and miscompiles quantum programs.
//
// Rebuild LLVM/MLIR with the patch tpls/customizations/llvm/mlir-ssi.diff
// applied (see scripts/build_llvm.sh). Do NOT work around this failure by
// editing the test or by adding a repair pass to the pipeline.

// Both successors of the branch are passed the same wire. The block arguments
// are information points (sigma nodes) and must survive.
// CHECK-LABEL: func.func @sigma_nodes_preserved(
// CHECK-SAME:    %[[C:.*]]: i1, %[[W:.*]]: !quake.wire)
// CHECK:         cf.cond_br %[[C]], ^bb1(%[[W]] : !quake.wire), ^bb2(%[[W]] : !quake.wire)
// CHECK:       ^bb1(%[[A:.*]]: !quake.wire):
// CHECK:         quake.x %[[A]]
// CHECK:       ^bb2(%[[B:.*]]: !quake.wire):
// CHECK:         quake.y %[[B]]
func.func @sigma_nodes_preserved(%c: i1, %w: !quake.wire) -> !quake.wire {
  cf.cond_br %c, ^bb1(%w : !quake.wire), ^bb2(%w : !quake.wire)
^bb1(%a: !quake.wire):
  %x = quake.x %a : (!quake.wire) -> !quake.wire
  cf.br ^bb3(%x : !quake.wire)
^bb2(%b: !quake.wire):
  %y = quake.y %b : (!quake.wire) -> !quake.wire
  cf.br ^bb3(%y : !quake.wire)
^bb3(%r: !quake.wire):
  return %r : !quake.wire
}
